// switchboard.templ — the layer switchboard (C-CALV4-LAYERS-P9, W-F).
//
// This is the surface three calendar-v4 waves reserved and none could build,
// because it needs a per-viewer PERSISTED store and no preference store of any
// kind existed in Chronicle. Migration 014 and POST /campaigns/:id/calendar/
// prefs are that store; this is what a viewer touches.
//
// WHY IT IS A TOP-LAYER [popover] AND THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL. `.cal-block-host
// .block` sets `overflow: hidden` and the host is a `container-type:
// inline-size` query container, so an absolutely-positioned box inside the
// Nameplate is clipped. The docked Ledger column was DELIBERATELY left a plain
// positioned box for exactly this ("it stays a plain positioned box so W-F's
// top-layer switchboard popover can still anchor inside it",
// calendar-block.css). The sheet is therefore a SIBLING of .block inside
// .cal-block-host: inside the query container for sizing, inside the scoping
// root every CSS test requires, and in the top layer when open.
//
// WHY EVERY ROW IS AN hx-post AND NOT A CSS REVEAL. Two independent reasons and
// either alone is fatal. First, TestSeam_EnabledLayerSetMatchesWhatRenders
// asserts that an ABSENT layer key produces an ABSENT surface — rendering all
// eight and hiding seven with CSS makes that test unfalsifiable, and its header
// calls the alternative "the set-and-ignored defect class this file exists to
// kill". Second, `needs backend` never renders to a player: emitting the
// horizon zone always and hiding it with CSS would put GM-facing honesty copy
// into a player's markup, and permission is ABSENCE, not `display: none`.
//
// WHY IT DOES NOT ANIMATE OPEN. Canon A8's L-M1 permits a section-opening
// animation and the signed mockup ships one (`.layer.opening` → @keyframes
// m-open). Chronicle's motion budget is four properties and one keyframe, all
// inside a reduced-motion guard, and `height` / `interpolate-size` / an
// `m-open` keyframe are outside it. The design notes already wrote the branch
// shipped here — "Under prefers-reduced-motion the section simply exists" — and
// Chronicle ships that branch for everyone ([LYR-9] item 1). The three
// transitions the ROW carries (.layerrow background, .sw background, .sw::after
// transform) ARE in the budget and do live inside the guard, which is itself
// the proof that the switch is a STATE and not an animation.

package calendar_block

// layerSheet is the switchboard: two groups, eight rows, one footer.
//
// [LYR-10] SIGNED: the two-group revision ships over the flat signed list. The
// rubric lines are canon A8's two laws written where the viewer meets them, and
// the footer states the per-viewer promise the whole slice exists to keep.
//
// THE DENOMINATOR NEVER VARIES BY ROLE. Eight rows and "of 8" for owner, co-DM
// and player alike; only the on-set differs. Filtering the row set by what a
// viewer would actually get reintroduces a permission dimension inside a
// display-preference registry, which REVIEW killed by name — and [LYR-6a]
// signs the row staying: a layer is a preference, not a promise of content, the
// same way a calendar with no eras gives an empty `eras` row.
templ layerSheet(d BlockData) {
	if switchboardLive(d.Layers) {
		<div
			popover
			id={ layerSheetID(d) }
			class="lsheet"
			style={ layerSheetAnchorStyle(d) }
			role="group"
			aria-label="Layers"
		>
			<div class="pop-bar" aria-hidden="true"></div>
			<div class="pop-h">{ layerSheetHeading(d.Layers) }</div>
			<div class="grp">
				<span class="g">In the month</span>
				<span class="how">instant · no confirm, no animation</span>
			</div>
			for _, r := range layerRowsInside() {
				@layerSheetRow(d, r)
			}
			<div class="grp">
				<span class="g">Below the month</span>
				<span class="how">opens a section</span>
			</div>
			for _, r := range layerRowsBelow() {
				@layerSheetRow(d, r)
			}
			<div class="pop-f">
				Your view only — remembered per viewer. A calendar starts as a calendar:
				the default is the month with its moon phases and nothing else.
			</div>
		</div>
	}
}

// layerSheetRow is ONE registry row, and it is a real, focusable,
// keyboard-reachable control — the signed <button data-layer-pick> primitive
// (calendar-v4.html:1876). REVIEW failed the proposed file for making it a
// <div>, and a <div> would also be invisible to the keyboard.
//
// Guard B3: controls end in `-pick` and never reuse an <html> state-marker
// noun. `data-layer-pick` is the mockup's own name and is clean.
//
// IT POSTS THE RESULTING SET, NOT A DELTA (see layerToggleVals): idempotent
// under a double click, no read-modify-write on the server, and — the reason
// that decides it — the SET is what the seam test compares against what
// renders. hx-vals is plain JSON, never `js:`-prefixed, because boot.js
// disables eval globally. hx-swap="none" because the answer is 204 with no
// body; the page rebuilds via HX-Refresh through its own already-gated handler.
//
// No CSRF field: boot.js attaches X-CSRF-Token from the cookie to every HTMX
// mutating request, globally and outside this widget (r54 §5).
templ layerSheetRow(d BlockData, r LayerRow) {
	<button
		type="button"
		class="layerrow"
		data-layer-pick={ r.Key }
		aria-pressed={ layerRowPressed(d.Layers, r.Key) }
		hx-post={ d.Layers.PersistURL }
		hx-vals={ layerToggleVals(d.Layers, r.Key) }
		hx-swap="none"
	>
		<span class="sw" aria-hidden="true"></span>
		<span class="tx">
			<span class="nm">{ r.Name }</span>
			<span class="wh">{ r.Where }</span>
		</span>
	</button>
}
